## Completed Experiments (one-line summaries)
- Iter 1: LightGBM genre-only → 0.8679. Genres beat mean baseline; 8/16 genres zero importance.
- Iter 2: LightGBM freq-encoded crew → 0.8689. Crew dominates importance; freq enc collapses rare entities.
- Iter 3: LightGBM OOF target-enc (smooth=10) → 0.8725. Director #1. High fold variance.
- Iter 4: LightGBM target-enc+curated flags → 0.8572. Curated flags proved genuine signal.
- Iter 5: XGBoost target-enc+curated flags → 0.8440. XGBoost > LightGBM on same features.
- Iter 6: Ridge freq-enc+curated flags (alpha=10) → 0.8522. Lowest variance (std=0.015).
- Iter 7: XGBoost regularized (depth=3, LR=0.03) → 0.8467. Shallower trees underfit.
- Iter 8: LightGBM native cat (mcs=5) → 0.8179. Breakthrough — native categorical handling.
- Iter 9: LightGBM native cat grid (nl=31, mcs=3) → 0.8097. BEST. Tight grid search confirmed.
- Iter 10: LightGBM native cat LR sweep (0.01, 0.02) → 0.8097 still best. Feature ceiling confirmed.
- Iter 11: LightGBM native cat + film counts → 0.8229. Film counts collinear; hurt performance.

## Learnings
- LightGBM native categorical (category dtype) beats all manual encoding by large margin (0.8097 vs 0.8440 best target-enc)
- Director is #1 feature by gain in all tree models; curated flags rank 3rd-5th consistently
- min_child_samples=3 better than 5 or 10; allows learning from rare crew combinations
- num_leaves=31 optimal; wider trees (63, 127) overfit on 675-row dataset
- lr=0.05 optimal; lower LR with more trees converges to same RMSE
- Film count features hurt — correlated with categorical identity, cause overfitting
- Ridge (Iter 6) has lowest fold variance (std=0.015) — best stacking base
- XGBoost (Iter 5) has different error patterns from LightGBM — good stacking diversity
- Feature ceiling reached: further gains require ensemble/stacking rather than new features
- Realistic ceiling post-stacking: ~0.79-0.80 RMSE
